        <body>
  <h1>Student Rights & Discipline in District 31: A Guide for Parents</h1>

  <p>In District 31 (Staten Island), encompassing neighborhoods like St. George, Tottenville, Great Kills, and New Dorp, students, particularly those with disabilities, possess specific rights regarding school discipline. We are dedicated to ensuring a fair, equitable, and supportive learning environment for *all* students within District 31.</p>

  <h2>Understanding Discipline and Special Education in NYS</h2>

  <p>New York State Education Law and the federal Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) provide crucial protections for students with disabilities facing disciplinary actions.  It's imperative for parents to be aware of these rights to effectively advocate for their children. Key principles include:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Due Process:</b>  Parents have the right to be notified of disciplinary actions and to challenge them. This includes the right to a hearing (see Due Process section below).</li>
    <li><b>Fairness and Equity:</b> Discipline must be applied fairly and without discrimination. Disciplinary policies should be clearly defined and consistently enforced.</li>
    <li><b>Least Restrictive Environment (LRE):</b> Disciplinary actions should be the least restrictive necessary to address the behavior, considering the student's individual needs. This means exploring alternative disciplinary measures before resorting to suspension or expulsion.  (See 8 NYCRR §200.1(cc)).</li>
    <li><b>IEP Implementation:</b> The school is obligated to implement the student's Individualized Education Program (IEP) fully and consistently.  Failures in IEP implementation can be a significant factor in disciplinary issues.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDR): A Critical Safeguard</h2>

  <p>If a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a 504 plan (if the behavior is related to the disability) faces a suspension exceeding 10 school days (whether consecutive or cumulative within a school year, constituting a "change in placement" under federal law), a Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) <b>must</b> be conducted within 10 school days of the decision to change the student’s placement because of a violation of a code of student conduct (34 CFR §300.530-536). This review is a critical process to determine if the student's behavior was:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Directly caused by the student's disability; <i>or</i></li>
      <li>A direct result of the school's failure to implement the student's IEP (or 504 plan).</li>
    </ol>

  <p><b>Who Participates in an MDR?</b>  The MDR team includes (34 CFR §300.530(e)):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The parent(s)/guardian(s).</li>
      <li>Relevant members of the IEP team, as determined by the parent and the school district. This *must* include someone knowledgeable about the student, the disability, and the IEP.</li>
    </ul>

  <p><b>What Happens During an MDR?</b>  The MDR team analyzes all relevant information in the student's file, including (34 CFR §300.530(e)):</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The student's IEP (or 504 plan).</li>
      <li>Teacher observations.</li>
      <li>Any relevant information provided by the parents.</li>
    </ul>

  <p><b>The Outcome of the MDR:</b></p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>If the behavior *was* a manifestation of the disability *or* due to IEP implementation failure:</b> The student returns to their current placement (unless parents and the school agree otherwise), and the IEP team must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) (if one hasn't been done already) and implement (or revise, if one exists) a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP).  This plan must be designed to address the problematic behavior. The district must also take steps to remedy the IEP implementation failures.</li>
      <li><b>If the behavior was *not* a manifestation of the disability *and* not due to IEP implementation failure:</b>  The student can be disciplined in the same manner as students without disabilities (for the same infraction), *except* that the school must continue to provide educational services so the student can continue to participate in the general education curriculum (though in another setting) and continue to progress toward meeting IEP goals.  (34 CFR §300.530(d)).</li>
    </ul>

  <p><b>Crucial Point:</b>  The burden of proof in an MDR rests on the <i>school district</i>.  They must demonstrate that the behavior was not related to the disability or their failure to implement the IEP.</p>

  <h2>Advice for Navigating Suspensions in District 31: A Step-by-Step Guide</h2>

  <p>Facing a suspension for your child, particularly a long-term one, can be overwhelming.  Here's a step-by-step guide to protect your child's rights:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><b>Immediate Actions:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Stay Calm:</b>  It's natural to be upset, but maintaining composure will help you advocate effectively.</li>
        <li><b>Request Information:</b> Immediately request a written copy of the disciplinary charges against your child and *all* supporting evidence (incident reports, witness statements, etc.).  Under New York State Education Law, you are entitled to this information.</li>
        <li><b>Document Everything:</b> Start a file.  Keep detailed records of all communications with the school (dates, times, who you spoke with, and a summary of the conversation).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li><b>If Your Child Has an IEP or 504 Plan:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Demand an MDR:</b>  Formally request a Manifestation Determination Review <i>immediately</i>.  Do this in writing and keep a copy. Time is of the essence. Cite 34 CFR §300.530.</li>
        <li><b>Review the IEP/504 Plan:</b> Carefully review your child's IEP or 504 plan to ensure it is being properly implemented.  Note any areas where the school is failing to provide required services or accommodations.  This will be crucial evidence for the MDR.</li>
        <li><b>Attend the MDR Meeting Prepared:</b> Bring documentation supporting your position (e.g., evidence of IEP implementation failures, doctor's notes, independent evaluations).  Bring a trusted advocate or special education attorney if possible.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li><b>Attending Meetings with School Officials:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Preparation is Key:</b> Before any meeting, prepare a list of questions and concerns. Review the disciplinary charges and supporting evidence thoroughly.</li>
        <li><b>Active Participation:</b>  Actively participate in the meeting.  Ask questions, present your perspective, and challenge any inaccurate or misleading information.</li>
        <li><b>Bring an Advocate:</b> Consider bringing a special education advocate or attorney to any disciplinary meetings.  They can provide invaluable support and ensure your child's rights are protected. The NYC DOE has an obligation to inform you of your right to an advocate.</li>
        <li><b>Take Detailed Notes:</b>  Take detailed notes of everything discussed during the meeting, including who said what.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li><b>Understanding Suspension Procedures in District 31:</b>
         <ul>
            <li><b>Short-Term Suspension (5 days or less):</b> You have the right to be informed of the reason for the suspension and to have an informal conference with the principal or other school official.</li>
            <li><b>Long-Term Suspension (more than 5 days):</b> You have the right to a formal hearing before a superintendent's designee.  This hearing must meet specific due process requirements (see Due Process section below).</li>
         </ul>
    </li>

    <li><b>The Importance of Documentation:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Maintain a Comprehensive File:</b> Keep copies of all correspondence, disciplinary notices, IEPs, 504 plans, medical records, evaluations, and any other relevant documents.</li>
        <li><b>Document Phone Calls:</b>  Keep a log of all phone calls with school officials, including the date, time, who you spoke with, and a summary of the conversation.</li>
        <li><b>Follow Up in Writing:</b>  After any meeting or phone call, send a follow-up email or letter summarizing what was discussed and confirming any agreements that were made.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>

    <li><b>Appealing Suspension Decisions:</b>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Know Your Appeal Rights:</b> You have the right to appeal a suspension decision to the Superintendent of Schools.  The specific procedures for filing an appeal will be outlined in the suspension notice.</li>
        <li><b>File the Appeal Promptly:</b>  There are strict deadlines for filing an appeal.  Make sure you file your appeal within the specified timeframe.</li>
        <li><b>Clearly State Your Grounds for Appeal:</b> In your appeal, clearly state the reasons why you believe the suspension was unfair or inappropriate.  Provide any supporting documentation. This can include challenging procedural errors, discriminatory practices, or that the punishment did not fit the crime.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Due Process Rights: Your Right to a Hearing</h2>

  <p>If your child faces a long-term suspension (more than 5 days), you have the right to a due process hearing. This hearing must meet certain requirements under New York State Education Law and IDEA, including:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><b>Written Notice:</b> You must receive written notice of the charges against your child, the date, time, and location of the hearing, and your rights at the hearing.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Counsel:</b> You have the right to be represented by an attorney or advocate at the hearing.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Present Evidence:</b> You have the right to present evidence on your child's behalf, including witness testimony and documents.</li>
    <li><b>Right to Cross-Examine Witnesses:</b> You have the right to cross-examine any witnesses who testify against your child.</li>
    <li><b>Impartial Hearing Officer:</b> The hearing must be conducted by an impartial hearing officer.</li>
    <li><b>Written Decision:</b> You are entitled to a written decision from the hearing officer explaining the reasons for their decision.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common District 31 Pitfalls to Watch Out For:</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Failure to Conduct Timely MDRs:</b> Districts sometimes delay or fail to conduct MDRs within the required timeframe (10 school days).  Be proactive and insist on a timely MDR.</li>
      <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b> Disciplinary issues often stem from a failure to properly implement the IEP.  Thoroughly document any instances of non-compliance.</li>
      <li><b>Insufficient FBAs/BIPs:</b>  If a student's behavior is a manifestation of their disability, the district must conduct a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA) and implement a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP).  These plans must be individualized and data-driven. Vague or generic plans are often ineffective.</li>
      <li><b>Lack of Parent Involvement:</b>  Parents are essential members of the IEP team and the MDR team.  Districts sometimes fail to adequately involve parents in these processes. Insist on being an active participant.</li>
      <li><b>Discriminatory Discipline:</b> Be vigilant for any signs of discriminatory discipline, where students with disabilities are disciplined more harshly than students without disabilities for similar offenses.</li>
    </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQ)</h2>

  <dl>
    <dt><b>Q: What is the difference between a suspension and an expulsion?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: A suspension is a temporary removal from school, while an expulsion is a permanent removal.  Expulsions are rare in New York State, particularly for students with disabilities.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What if my child's behavior is dangerous?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: Even if a student's behavior is dangerous, the school must still follow proper procedures, including conducting an MDR if the student has an IEP or 504 plan. The school can pursue an interim alternative educational setting (IAES) for up to 45 school days under specific circumstances (e.g., weapons possession, drug offenses, or inflicting serious bodily injury). (34 CFR §300.530(g)). A hearing officer can also order a change of placement for up to 45 days if the student poses a danger. (34 CFR §300.532).</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: Can my child be suspended for behaviors that occur off-campus?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: In some cases, yes. If the off-campus behavior has a direct and substantial impact on the school environment or the safety of students or staff, the school may be able to take disciplinary action. The incident must be related to the school environment in some way. This area is often a gray area, and you should seek expert counsel if facing this situation.</dd>

    <dt><b>Q: Where can I find the regulations you mentioned?</b></dt>
    <dd>A:
         <ul>
            <li><b>IDEA Regulations (34 CFR Part 300):</b>  Available on the U.S. Department of Education website.</li>
            <li><b>New York State Part 200 Regulations (8 NYCRR Part 200):</b> Available on the New York State Education Department (NYSED) website.</li>
            <li><b>NYSED Discipline Guidance:</b> Search for "NYSED discipline guidance students with disabilities" on the NYSED website.</li>
         </ul>
    </dd>

    <dt><b>Q: What if my child doesn't have an IEP or 504 plan, but I suspect they have a disability?</b></dt>
    <dd>A: You have the right to request an evaluation to determine if your child has a disability. Contact the school's special education department or your child's guidance counselor to initiate the evaluation process. It is best to do this in writing so you have a record of your request.  Document the date and keep a copy. The school must respond in a reasonable timeframe.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>Local Resources in District 31</h2>

    <ul>
        <li><b>District 31 Community Education Council (CEC):</b> Connect with other parents and advocate for improved educational outcomes.</li>
        <li><b>INCLUDEnyc:</b> A New York City-based organization providing resources and support for families of children with disabilities.  They offer workshops and legal clinics.</li>
    </ul>

</body>
